###  https://ark.adaptcentre.ie/Ontologies/ARKHealthTerminology#StrategicRisk
:StrategicRisk rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                        <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos#Concept> ;
               <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "07/09/2020" ;
               <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> "Rob" ;
               <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "HSE Part 1/ISO 31000:2009" ;
               rdfs:comment "A strategic risk has the ability to impact on the achievement/delivery of the HSE’s strategic objectives/directions. Strategic risks relate to the highest level of objective for the HSE, which typically have a long-term focus and are linked to the HSE’s Strategic Plan." ;
               rdfs:label "Strategic Risk" ;
               <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos#definition> "A strategic risk has the ability to impact on the achievement/delivery of the HSE’s strategic objectives/directions. Strategic risks relate to the highest level of objective for the HSE, which typically have a long-term focus and are linked to the HSE’s Strategic Plan." ;
               <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos#prefLabel> "Strategic Risk" .


###  https://ark.adaptcentre.ie/Ontologies/ARKHealthTerminology#SuspectedHarmEvent
:SuspectedHarmEvent rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos#Concept> ;
                    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "18/12/2020" ;
                    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> "Lucy" ;
                    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "HSE Incident Management Framework - Guidance 2020" ;
                    rdfs:comment "A suspected harm event is an incident which is an event or circumstance which could have or did lead to unintended and/or unnecessary harm (WHO)" ;
                    rdfs:label "Suspected Harm Event" ;
                    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos#definition> "A suspected harm event is an incident which is an event or circumstance which could have or did lead to unintended and/or unnecessary harm (WHO)" ;
                    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos#prefLabel> "Suspected Harm Event" .


###  https://ark.adaptcentre.ie/Ontologies/ARKHealthTerminology#SystemsAnalysis
:SystemsAnalysis rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos#Concept> ;
                 <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "18/12/2020" ;
                 <http://purl.org/dc/terms/creator> "Lucy" ;
                 <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "HSE Incident Management Framework 2020" ;
                 rdfs:comment "A methodical review of an incident which involves the collection of data from the literature, records (general records in the case of non-clinical incidents and healthcare records in the case of clinical incidents), individual interviews with those involved where the incident occurred and analysis of this data to establish the chronology of events that led up to the incident, identifying findings that the reviewers considered had an effect on the eventual harm, the contributory factors, and recommended control actions to address the contributory factors to prevent future harm arising as far as is reasonably practicable. The principles of systems analysis can be applied using a comprehensive, concise or aggregate approach." ;
                 rdfs:label "Systems Analysis" ;
                 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos#definition> "A methodical review of an incident which involves the collection of data from the literature, records (general records in the case of non-clinical incidents and healthcare records in the case of clinical incidents), individual interviews with those involved where the incident occurred and analysis of this data to establish the chronology of events that led up to the incident, identifying findings that the reviewers considered had an effect on the eventual harm, the contributory factors, and recommended control actions to address the contributory factors to prevent future harm arising as far as is reasonably practicable. The principles of systems analysis can be applied using a comprehensive, concise or aggregate approach." ;
                 <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos#prefLabel> "Systems Analysis" .


